   {
    "heading": "Twitch's username rules",
    "content": "<ul><li><strong>Length:</strong> 4 to 25 characters.</li><li><strong>Characters:</strong> letters, numbers and underscores only. No spaces, hyphens, full stops or emoji, so <em>Copper Otter</em> becomes <em>CopperOtter</em> or <em>Copper_Otter</em>.</li><li><strong>Capitals:</strong> your channel address is your username in lower case (twitch.tv/copperottertv). Your display name can add capital letters to make it easier to read.</li><li><strong>Changing it:</strong> you can change your username once every 60 days, but not while you're live or while a video is uploading.</li><li><strong>Taken names:</strong> Twitch doesn't release inactive usernames, so a name someone registered years ago usually stays taken.</li></ul><p>Every name this generator makes fits those rules, and none starts with an underscore. Rules can change, so if a name won't go through, check Twitch's own help pages.</p>"
   },
   {
    "heading": "How streamer names work",
    "content": "<p>A Twitch name has to work in three places: said out loud on stream, typed in chat and read in a list of live channels. The names that stick tend to follow a few patterns:</p><ul><li><strong>A brand plus a channel word.</strong> TV, Live, Streams, Plays or HQ on the end makes a name sound like a show, and it's the usual way to get a name when the plain version is taken.</li><li><strong>Short and easy to type.</strong> Viewers tag you with @ and type your name to shout you out, so one or two short words beat a long phrase.</li><li><strong>A mood.</strong> Cosy streams have soft, homely names; competitive ones sound sharp. The name is the first hint of what the stream is like.</li><li><strong>A joke about yourself.</strong> Funny names that admit you're bad at something give chat a running joke from day one.</li></ul><p>Read your name in lower case before you commit, since that's how it appears in your channel address: two words run together can spell something you didn't mean. Every name here has been checked for that.</p>"
   },
   {
    "heading": "Check it's free before you go live",
    "content": "<p>The names here are free to use, but you need to check each one is available. Search twitch.tv/ followed by the name, or try it in your account settings. If you're building a channel, check the same handle on YouTube, TikTok and Instagram too, so viewers can find you everywhere under one name, and if the channel might grow into a brand, search the trade mark registers (the UK IPO or USPTO) as well. This isn't legal advice.</p><p>For the rest of your setup, try the <a href=\"youtube-channel-name-generator.html\">YouTube Channel Name Generator</a> for a matching channel and the <a href=\"discord-server-name-generator.html\">Discord Server Name Generator</a> for your community server. Gaming on console? The <a href=\"xbox-gamertag-generator.html\">Xbox Gamertag Generator</a> and <a href=\"psn-name-generator.html\">PSN Name Generator</a> follow those platforms' rules, and the <a href=\"gamertag-generator.html\">Gamertag Generator</a> makes names for anywhere.</p>"
